[38;5;231m⏺[39m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m it is.

  One more structural question, then I'll lay out approaches. [1mThe fork:[0m how a shared module actually reaches [38;5;153mapp.js[39m,
  which today is a bare [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m with no module system.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m does [38;5;153mimport { ... } from './src/session.js'[39m. Standard,
    explicit dependencies, no global namespace, and each module is independently testable. Cost: browsers block module
    imports over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking stops working — you'd need a local static server
    ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m, one line). Also note [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m uses CommonJS ([38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m) for Node, so the repo
    would have two module styles coexisting unless we convert.
  - [1mPlain scripts + a global[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="src/session.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and hang the API off a namespace like
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSession[39m. Zero tooling, [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working. Cost: load order becomes an invisible dependency, and the
    global namespace is exactly what gets messy once "other forms" arrive.
  - [1mA bundler[0m — correct at scale, and unjustifiable for six files. I'd rule this out under YAGNI.

  This one is genuinely hard to change later: whichever you pick, every future form inherits it, and converting a grown
  app between the two is tedious.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m ES modules. The thing you described — a shared module several forms depend on — is the case module
  syntax exists for, and losing [38;5;153mfile://[39m costs one command to fix. I'd leave [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m on CommonJS
  for now rather than expand scope; the new session module is browser-only anyway, since [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m doesn't exist in
  Node.
